.Dd November 19, 2026
.Dt SAFE-STORE 5
.Os POSIX.1-2017
.Sh NAME
.Nm safe-store
.Nd encrypted file storage for your secrets
.Sh DESCRIPTION
This is a fork of Willy Goiffon's
.Xr safe-store 5 ;
see
.Xr safe 1
for a summary of the changes.
.Pp
The
.Nm
is a directory containing encrypted files called
.Em secrets .
There can be any number of sub-directories; a secret is referred to by
its path relative to the
.Nm .
.Bd -literal
  $ find .secrets -type f
  .secrets/master
  .secrets/name
  .secrets/subdir/name
  .secrets/subdir/othername
.Ed
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Pa .secrets
Default
.Nm
path.
.It Pa .secrets/cipher
An arbitrary secret named "cipher".
.It Pa .secrets/master
A special secret used as the reference for deriving the key from the
master password.
.El
.Sh FORMAT
Each secret is the concatenation of a 40-byte CREAM header and a
secretstream payload produced by
.Xr crypto_secretstream_xchacha20poly1305 3
(XChaCha20-Poly1305).
The key is derived once, with Argon2id, from the master password and
the salt recorded in the
.Pa master
header, and is reused for every secret in the store.
The salt recorded in a non-master secret's header is a copy of the
master's salt; it is not used to derive the key.
.Pp
The header is laid out as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 6 bytes
Magic
.Dq CREAM\e1 .
.It 2 bytes
Format version, network byte order.
.It 4 bytes
Secretstream chunk size, network byte order.
The only accepted value is 8192; any other value is rejected at
parse time.
.It 4 bytes
Argon2id memory limit in KiB, network byte order.
.It 4 bytes
Argon2id time cost, network byte order.
.It 4 bytes
Argon2id parallelism, network byte order.  Ignored by libsodium, but
validated for exact equality with the expected value.
.It 16 bytes
Argon2id salt.
.El
.Pp
The header layout and the key-derivation parameters are frozen.
Readers validate the magic, chunk size and Argon2id parameters for
exact equality with the values below, and accept the two versions
below; the salt is read but not validated.
A file whose header records any other value is rejected at parse time,
before any decryption is attempted.
The expected values are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It version
0x12 (written), or 0x11 (read only).
.It chunk size
8192.
.It Argon2id memory
256 MiB (262144 KiB).
.It Argon2id time
3.
.It Argon2id parallelism
1.
.El
.Sh VERSIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 0x12
Written by this version of
.Xr safe 1
and
.Xr safe-agent 1
for every new entry, including a new
.Pa master .
The associated data of every chunk is the 40-byte header followed by
the entry's name, so no header field, the version and the salt copy
included, can be changed without the entry failing to decrypt.
The final chunk is padded; see
.Sx PADDING .
.It 0x11
The previous format: the associated data is the name alone, and there
is no padding.
Entries in this format are still read, and are left as they are; an
entry becomes 0x12 when it is next written, for example with
.Nm safe Fl f Fl a .
.El
.Pp
A store may hold entries of both versions.
Programs from before 0x12 reject 0x12 entries, and a store whose
.Pa master
is 0x12 (every store created by this version) can not be opened by
them at all.
.Sh PADDING
In format 0x12 only the final chunk is padded.
Its plaintext, r bytes, is extended with
.Xr sodium_pad 3
(a 0x80 byte, then zero bytes, as in ISO/IEC 7816-4) to the smallest
power of two that is at least 256 and greater than r.
Since a chunk holds at most 8192 bytes, r is at most 8191: when the
plaintext ends exactly at a chunk boundary, it is followed by an empty
final chunk, padded to 256 bytes.
Every chunk before the final one holds exactly 8192 bytes.
.Pp
An entry of n bytes therefore takes
.Bd -literal -offset indent
40 + 24 + floor(n / 8192) * (8192 + 17) + b + 17
.Ed
.Pp
bytes on disk, where b is the padded size of the remaining
n mod 8192 bytes.
All entries shorter than 256 bytes, which covers practically every
password, have the same size, 337 bytes.
Longer entries reveal their length rounded up to a power of two up to
8 KiB, and above that their length to the nearest 8 KiB.
.Pp
Readers remove the padding after the final chunk has been
authenticated and accept only the padding a writer produces: the
padded size must be the power of two that the unpadded length calls
for, and the padding must be exactly 0x80 followed by zero bytes.
.Pp
Entry names are not padded or hidden; see
.Sx SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS .
.Sh PORTABILITY
The chunk size recorded in the header is the fixed value 8192,
independent of the platform where
.Xr safe 1
was built.
Stores are interchangeable across libcs and architectures; a store
created by a binary built against one libc can be opened by a binary
built against another.
.Pp
Newer versions of safe read every format version listed under
.Sx VERSIONS ;
older versions read only the versions they knew.
.Sh TRUNCATION
Readers require that the final chunk of the secretstream be tagged
.Dv TAG_FINAL ,
that every chunk before it be tagged as an ordinary message, and that
no bytes follow it.
This prevents truncation attacks at buffer boundaries and the appending
of arbitrary data.
.Sh WRITES ARE ATOMIC
New secrets, and the master entry, are written to a temporary file in
the same directory and then atomically renamed into place, followed by
an
.Xr fsync 2
of the file and of the containing directory.
Without
.Fl f ,
the final step uses rename-without-replace
.Pq Xr renameat2 2 No on Linux, link+unlink elsewhere ,
so an existing secret is never overwritten.
.Pp
A crash mid-write leaves the previous secret, if any, untouched.
.Sh INDEPENDENT STORES
Each
.Nm
directory has its own
.Pa master
entry, its own Argon2id salt, and its own key.
Two stores on the same machine (for example the default-profile
.Pa ~/.secrets
and the hardened
.Pa /var/lib/safe/browser )
are cryptographically independent: compromise of one does not
compromise the other, and losing one master password does not affect
the other.
The agents that serve them are separate processes with separate
retained keys; the agent never returns its key, only decrypted or
encrypted entries.
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
The master secret is used to verify that the key derived from your
master password is correct.
Every secret in a store is encrypted under the same key, so the same
salt and Argon2id parameters; a secret whose header records different
parameters is rejected.
.Pp
Entry names are file names and are not encrypted, and the number of
entries and their sizes, rounded as described under
.Sx PADDING ,
are visible to anyone who can list the store directory.
Keep the store, and every copy of it, on encrypted storage if that
matters.
.Pp
The bytes in
.Pa master
are never interpreted, only decrypted; any secret encrypted with the
correct master password and with
.Pa master
as its authenticated name works.
.Pp
To recover from a corrupt master entry, the header (which holds the
salt) must be intact.
If the header is also corrupt, copy any decryptable secret over
.Pa master
to restore a valid header, then re-encrypt the entry in place:
.Bd -literal
  cd $SAFE_DIR
  cp random_entry master
  chmod 400 master
  safe -f -a master < /dev/null
  password:
.Ed
.Pp
The password prompt takes the existing master password; the bytes read
from stdin become the new master entry's payload and are never
interpreted, so any content works.
Do not rename an encrypted file to
.Pa master
without re-encrypting it: because the secret name is authenticated as
AEAD associated data, the rename makes the file undecryptable under
its new name.
.Pp
Do not delete
.Pa master
unless the entire store has been backed up and is intended to be
abandoned.
The salt in
.Pa master
is the only copy used for key derivation; without it no secret can
be decrypted, and there is no recovery path.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr safe 1 ,
.Xr safe-agent 1
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Willy Goiffon Aq Mt dev@z3bra.org
.Pp
Modifications in this fork: see
.Pa README .